# Every text file keeps LF endings in the worktree, not only in the index. The repository has no
# binary files at all today; `text=auto` still lets git decide, so adding one changes nothing here.
#
# Windows checkouts here run with core.autocrlf=true, which gave every file a CRLF worktree copy.
# Nothing on Windows minded. But the development container (scripts/devbox.sh) mounts this same
# worktree and runs Linux tools over the same bytes, and two things then go wrong:
#
#   - Linux bash stops on the first line of a CRLF script with `set: pipefail: invalid option
#     name`, because the option it read was `pipefail\r` — a message that names nothing it is
#     about. Git Bash's own bash tolerates CRLF, which is why it went unnoticed for so long.
#   - core.autocrlf lives in the Windows *system* gitconfig, which the container's git never reads.
#     Without this file, git in the box saw 413 files as modified against their LF blobs, which made
#     `git add -A` in there a trap and made `scripts/release.sh` refuse to run at all — it requires
#     a clean tree, and the box is the only place on this machine where the gate can run. Neither
#     `* text=auto` nor core.autocrlf alone fixes that; pinning `eol=lf` does.
#
# Adding the attribute is not enough on its own: a worktree that already holds CRLF copies keeps
# them until the files are checked out again (`git add --renormalize . && git checkout -- .`).
* text=auto eol=lf
